Data may be created as part of an organization’s normal operations, collected specifically to answer a question, or obtained from an outside source.


Organizations routinely create data through their operations, including:

sales transactions and inventory records;
customer, employee, payroll, and accounting systems;
website traffic and marketing-platform activity;
patient encounters and appointment systems; and
student enrollment and course records.

These data were often created to run a process rather than to support the analyst’s current question. Definitions, missing values, repeated records, and changes in business rules require careful review.



Surveys, experiments, interviews, observations, and measurements can be designed specifically for a research or business question. Purpose-generated data provide more control over what is measured, but collection choices can introduce sampling, nonresponse, measurement, or response bias.



Analysts also reuse data collected by governments, nonprofits, researchers, vendors, and public platforms. Examples include the Census Bureau, economic agencies, transportation departments, and open-data portals. Before using an external dataset, inspect its provenance, definitions, time coverage, license, and update schedule.

