Report spread with context
A useful written result includes:

the variable and unit;
the population or sample being described;
the center and spread;
the number of nonmissing observations; and
any meaningful skew, gaps, or unusual observations visible in a graph.

Example 3: relationships





Listed price and guest capacity for Chicago listings priced at $500 or less




A reasonable interpretation is that listings with greater capacity tend to have higher listed prices, while prices still vary greatly at the same capacity. This is an association, not proof that increasing capacity causes price to rise.

What the scatterplot does not establish

whether the pattern is statistically reliable in a larger population;
whether omitted variables explain the relationship;
whether price was actually paid;
whether the source snapshot represents another market or season; or
whether the relationship remains after accounting for room type and neighborhood.

Evaluate a published survey
Before trusting the headline, look for:

the target population and dates;
the sampling or recruitment method;
sample size and, when applicable, margin of sampling error;
response rate and evidence about nonresponse;
the exact question wording and response choices;
survey mode;
weighting variables and benchmarks;
sponsor and field organization; and
limitations stated by the researchers.







TipAcknowledge, then respond



Naming a possible bias is the start of analysis, not the end. Explain its likely direction, whether it threatens the specific conclusion, what was done to reduce it, and what additional evidence would help.
